			<div class="content" id="post-content-4475829">
									

<p>I don't quite follow where this links into Alexandria Eternal.</p>
<p>I had a few issues with this. I wasn't a fan of the exploration log sections - I found the MTF characters forgettable (after reading, I didn't retain any sense of who was who or what their personalities were like), and as a result just shrugged when one unfortunate guy got knocked off. There were also some instances of not very clinical descriptions and a few SPAG errors.</p>
<p>Unless I missed it, maybe with the Alexandria link, I also noticed there didn't seem to be much relevance of this huge ancient advanced spaceship to anything in the real world. That's not necessarily a problem - 'the ancient aliens did this thing' has been done a billion times - I was just expecting some bigger connection to the real world or Foundation lore to materialise that never came.</p>
<p>Having said all that, I still liked this. It has a neat classic sci-fi adventure feel, without being too derivative and incorporating some interesting themes about faith and loyalty. I think the core idea of an alien race that uses a cognitohazardous language is great - it's original, yet still sounds like something Clarke would have written about, and is simple and easy to understand while still being a distinctly SCP-flavoured thing. +1</p>

							</div>

								
					
