					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gentlegifts" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4275201); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4275201&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795048" alt="GentleGifts" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4275201)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gentlegifts" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4275201); return false;" >GentleGifts</a></span> <span class="odate time_1579662301 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Jan 2020 03:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4475858">
									

<p>Where this links to 4001 is in the disconnect between what the wayfarers expect to find at their destination. They find an uninhabited world, but <em>Nine Solemn Wind</em> first found the world (Earth) because of the presence of a thriving civilization: <em>Homo nobilus</em>, and is very concerned and confused by what their records tell them.</p>
<p>The events of Alexandria Burning and the subsequent reset happen while they're en route to Earth.</p>
<p>The lack of greater consequence and influence is sort of the thematic point. The entire piece is a massive 'fuck you' to its central antagonist, and tyranny and facism in general. It's very deliberate, from the poem from which this piece draws its name, to the ending where Their Majesty's corpse is originally mistaken as just another lump of shrapnel. Their legacy is just a broken wreck lost in the desert.</p>
<p>I do plan on further revising this to clean up parts I'm not satisfied with. There's a lot of untapped potential here and I was rushing to hit the deadline. E10 are deliberately clinical and professional, but there's room to properly develop the familial aspects of P26.</p>

							</div>

								
